.DS_Store
__pycache__/
# Benchmark artifacts. Written into the repo root by `bench/run.ts`, so they land
# next to the working tree rather than in a scratch directory.
#
# Enumerated one by one until this change, which is the defect: the list named the
# three artifacts that existed when it was written and nothing since, so every
# artifact added later -- the recall curve, the gap attribution, the failure
# census, the TR classes, the skip record, and all three ablation report pairs --
# was committable. A measurement is not source, and committing one makes a
# figure that must be re-derivable look like a tracked fact; it also means a
# single `git add -A` after a local run silently ships the operator's numbers.
#
# A glob covers the whole family and, more importantly, covers the next artifact
# too -- which is what an enumeration cannot do, and what went wrong here.
benchmark-*
# ...and one negation, because a glob without one is the previous defect mirrored.
#
# `benchmark-*` carries no slash, so it matches at every depth -- including
# `.github/workflows/benchmark-cortex-memory.yml`, the workflow that runs the
# cognitive arm. The split commit added the file, `git add -A` reported nothing,
# and the workflow that a dispatch had just been pointed at did not exist in the
# repository. The enumeration missed the next artifact; the glob swallowed the next
# source file, and both are the same mistake: a rule about artifacts applied to a
# name instead of to a place.
#
# The negation is scoped to the one directory where a `benchmark-*` file is source,
# rather than narrowing the glob back to today's three suffixes -- narrowing would
# restore the original defect the moment a fourth artifact appears.
!.github/workflows/benchmark-*
# ...and a second negation, because the first one was scoped to **one directory** and there are four.
#
# The paragraph above states the rule it was trying to follow -- "a rule about artifacts applied to a
# name instead of to a place" -- and then applied it to one place. `**/__tests__/` is the other place a
# `benchmark-*` file is source, and three test files were invisible to git because of it:
#
#   packages/cortex-eval/src/__tests__/benchmark-concurrency.test.ts      (the byte-identity falsifier
#                                                                          §14.22's pool rests on)
#   packages/cortex-eval/src/__tests__/benchmark-raw-capture.test.ts
#   packages/cortex-eval/src/__tests__/benchmark-retrieval-capture.test.ts
#
# They ran locally, so `pnpm check` was green while CI never executed them -- the local-pass/CI-fail
# drift this repository pays a pre-push hook to prevent, arriving through an ignore rule rather than
# through a skipped test. `git status --ignored` is the command that finds this class; a plain
# `git status` reports a clean tree, which is what a defect that hides files looks like.
!**/__tests__/**
# Re-ignored **after** the negation, because the last matching pattern wins: `__pycache__/` above would
# otherwise be un-ignored for every test directory, and a compiled `.pyc` is not source.
__pycache__/
# Probe scratch. `tools/run-program.py --keep` writes improvised programs here so a
# probe survives the session that wrote it instead of living in scrollback.
#
# Ignored for the same reason as the benchmark artifacts, and for one more: a probe
# is evidence *for* a conclusion, not the conclusion, so a tracked probe would make
# a discarded line of investigation look like a supported claim. The directory is
# named rather than globbed because probes are always written here and nowhere else.
probe/

